from ot1 import s
s('annunciation','The Angel Visits Mary','NT',['Luke 1:26-38'],'🕊️',
 "In a town called Nazareth lived a girl named Mary. One day the angel Gabriel came to her. 'Hello, Mary! God is with you. You will have a baby, and you will call him Jesus. He is the Son of God.' Mary said, 'I am the Lord's servant. Let it be as you say.' Mary trusted God.",
 "Mary was a young woman in Nazareth, promised in marriage to Joseph. One day the angel Gabriel appeared and said, 'Greetings, favoured one! The Lord is with you.' Mary was surprised and wondered what this greeting meant. 'Do not be afraid, Mary. You will have a son and you will name him Jesus. He will be called the Son of the Most High, and his kingdom will never end.' Mary asked, 'How can this be?' The angel said the Holy Spirit would come upon her, 'for nothing is impossible with God.' Mary answered, 'I am the Lord's servant. May it be done to me as you say.' We say 'yes' like Mary in the prayer called the Angelus.",
 "Everything in the New Testament begins with a question and a 'yes'. Gabriel greets Mary with a title no one else is given, 'full of grace' (or 'favoured one'). Mary is troubled but not frightened away; she asks an honest question, and is told that the Holy Spirit will overshadow her, as the cloud overshadowed the tent of meeting in the desert. She is free to say no, and she says 'Let it be done to me according to your word.' Catholics pray the Hail Mary, which begins with Gabriel's greeting, and the Angelus, which remembers this moment three times a day. The Church calls Mary the new Eve: where Eve's 'no' brought sin into the world, Mary's 'yes' opens the way for Christ.",
 ['What did Mary say to the angel?','Why do you think Mary said yes?','How can you say yes to God today?'],'Luke 1:38',hue=215)

s('visitation','Mary Visits Elizabeth','NT',['Luke 1:39-56'],'💛',
 "Mary hurried to visit her cousin Elizabeth, who was going to have a baby too. When Mary said hello, Elizabeth's baby jumped for joy! Elizabeth said, 'You are blessed, Mary!' Mary sang a happy song to God: 'My soul praises the Lord.' We call it the Magnificat.",
 "After the angel's visit, Mary set off in a hurry to the hill country of Judea to see her older cousin Elizabeth, who was expecting a baby, John, after many years of waiting. As soon as Elizabeth heard Mary's greeting, the baby leapt in her womb and she was filled with the Holy Spirit. 'Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the child you carry!' she cried. 'Why am I so honoured that the mother of my Lord should come to me?' Mary responded with a song: 'My soul proclaims the greatness of the Lord, and my spirit rejoices in God my Saviour.' She stayed with Elizabeth about three months, helping her.",
 "Mary's first act after saying yes is to go in haste to help someone else. The meeting of two mothers is full of joy: John leaps in the womb, the first to recognise the Lord, and Elizabeth speaks the words that form the second half of the Hail Mary. Mary's song, the Magnificat, is sung at Evening Prayer every day by the Church. It echoes Hannah's song in the Old Testament and praises God who lifts up the lowly and fills the hungry with good things. She does not keep the credit for herself: 'He who is mighty has done great things for me.'",
 ['Why did Mary hurry to Elizabeth?','What does Mary’s song say about God?','Who could you visit or help this week?'],'Luke 1:46-47',hue=335)

s('birth','Jesus Is Born','NT',['Luke 2:1-20'],'⭐',
 "Mary and Joseph travelled to Bethlehem. There was no room at the inn. So Jesus was born in a stable, and Mary laid him in a manger. Out in the fields, angels sang to the shepherds, 'Good news! A Saviour is born!' The shepherds ran to see him. Jesus, God's own Son, was a tiny baby.",
 "The emperor ordered a count of all the people, so Joseph took Mary, who was about to have her baby, to Bethlehem, the city of David. The town was crowded and there was no room for them in the inn. There in a stable, Mary gave birth to her son. She wrapped him in cloths and laid him in a manger, a feeding trough for animals. Out on the hills, shepherds were watching their sheep at night when an angel appeared. 'Do not be afraid. I bring you good news of great joy for all people. Today a Saviour has been born to you.' A host of angels sang, 'Glory to God in the highest, and peace on earth!' The shepherds hurried to Bethlehem and found the baby, just as the angel said.",
 "Luke tells the greatest event in history with quiet details: a census, a crowded town, a feeding trough. God enters the world as a poor baby, born outside the inn. The first people to hear are not kings or priests but shepherds, ordinary workers who were not highly regarded. 'Bethlehem' means 'house of bread', and Jesus is laid in a manger, a place where food is put, hinting at the Eucharist. Mary 'treasured all these things and pondered them in her heart.' The Church celebrates this at Christmas Mass, and the angels' song, 'Glory to God in the highest', is sung every Sunday.",
 ['Why do you think God chose a stable and shepherds?','What did the angels say?','How can we make room for Jesus in our hearts?'],'Luke 2:11',hue=50)

s('magi','The Wise Men','NT',['Matthew 2:1-12'],'🎁',
 "Wise men saw a bright new star. They knew it meant a great king had been born. They followed it a long way. At last, they found Jesus with Mary. They knelt down and gave him presents: gold, frankincense and myrrh. Then they went home a different way. We celebrate this on a day called Epiphany.",
 "Far to the east, wise men who studied the stars saw a new star rise and knew it meant a new king of the Jews had been born. They travelled to Jerusalem and asked King Herod where to find him. Herod was frightened and jealous, and asked them to come back and tell him. The star led them to Bethlehem, where it stopped over the house. They saw the child with Mary his mother, and they knelt and worshipped him. They gave him gold, frankincense and myrrh. God warned them in a dream not to go back to Herod, so they went home another way.",
 "The Magi are the first people from outside Israel to worship Jesus, and we celebrate the feast of the Epiphany for them: the 'showing' of Christ to all nations. Their gifts have been seen as signs of who Jesus is: gold for a king, frankincense for God, and myrrh, used for burial, for the one who would die. Herod's fear is also real, and it leads to the flight to Egypt. The wise men show that God can be found by anyone who searches sincerely, and that finding him changes our road: they 'went home by another way'.",
 ['What gift would you bring to Jesus?','Why did the wise men go home another way?','Who helps lead you to Jesus, like the star?'],'Matthew 2:10',hue=265)

s('temple','Jesus in the Temple','NT',['Luke 2:41-52'],'🏛️',
 "When Jesus was twelve, he went with Mary and Joseph to Jerusalem. On the way home, they could not find him! They looked and looked. After three days, they found him in the Temple, talking with the teachers. Mary said, 'Why did you do this?' Jesus said, 'I must be in my Father's house.' Then he went home and obeyed his parents.",
 "Every year Mary and Joseph went to Jerusalem for the feast of Passover. When Jesus was twelve, he went with them. On the way home, they travelled with a big group of relatives and friends, and only after a whole day did they realise Jesus was not with them. Worried, they went back to Jerusalem. After three days they found him in the Temple, sitting among the teachers, listening and asking questions. Everyone was amazed at his understanding. Mary said, 'Son, why have you done this to us? We have been looking for you anxiously.' Jesus answered, 'Why were you looking for me? Did you not know I must be in my Father's house?' Then he went home with them and was obedient.",
 "This is the only story of Jesus' boyhood in the Gospels. It reveals two things at once: Jesus is truly God's Son, who knows the Temple is his Father's house, and he is truly a child who goes home with his parents and obeys them. Mary and Joseph do not fully understand, and Luke says that Mary 'treasured all these things in her heart'. The three days of searching foreshadow another three days of loss, after the cross. This is the fifth of the Joyful Mysteries of the Rosary, 'The Finding of the Child Jesus in the Temple'.",
 ['Why did Mary and Joseph look so long?','What did Jesus say about his Father’s house?','How can we show we love God’s house, the church?'],'Luke 2:52',hue=30)

s('baptism','Jesus Is Baptised','NT',['Matthew 3:13-17'],'💧',
 "Jesus went to the Jordan River, where John was baptising people. John said, 'You should baptise me!' But Jesus said, 'This is how we do what God wants.' John baptised Jesus. Then the sky opened. The Holy Spirit came down like a dove, and God said, 'This is my beloved Son. I am very pleased with him.'",
 "John the Baptist lived in the desert and told people to turn back to God and be baptised in the river Jordan as a sign of being sorry for their sins. One day Jesus came from Galilee to be baptised by John. John tried to stop him: 'I need to be baptised by you!' Jesus said, 'Let it be so now, for we must do all that God asks.' So John baptised him. As Jesus came up out of the water, the heavens opened and the Spirit of God came down on him like a dove, and a voice from heaven said, 'This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.' All three Persons of the Trinity are there: the Father speaks, the Son is baptised, and the Spirit comes as a dove.",
 "Jesus has no sin, so why is he baptised? He goes down into the waters to stand with sinners and to make the water holy for us. At the Jordan, the heavens open, the Spirit descends and the Father speaks: the first full revelation of the Holy Trinity in the Gospels. The words 'beloved Son' echo Isaac, and 'with whom I am well pleased' echo the servant in Isaiah. In our own baptism, we too are named God's beloved children. Catholics trace the sign of the cross and make a little renewal of baptism with holy water each time we enter a church.",
 ['Why did Jesus want to be baptised?','Who is in the story: Father, Son and Spirit?','What do you remember about your own baptism?'],'Matthew 3:17',hue=200)

s('disciples','Jesus Calls His Friends','NT',['Mark 1:16-20','Luke 5:1-11'],'🎣',
 "Some men were fishing by the lake. They had caught nothing all night! Jesus said, 'Throw your nets again.' Simon did, and the nets filled with so many fish they began to tear! Jesus said, 'Follow me. I will make you fishers of people.' They left their boats and followed Jesus.",
 "Simon, Andrew, James and John were fishermen on the Sea of Galilee. After a long night of fishing, they had caught nothing. Jesus was teaching a crowd on the shore and asked to use Simon's boat. When he finished, he said, 'Go out into deep water and let down your nets.' Simon said, 'Master, we worked hard all night and caught nothing, but because you say so, I will.' The nets became so full of fish that they began to tear, and other boats had to come to help. Simon fell at Jesus' knees: 'Go away from me, Lord, for I am a sinful man!' Jesus said, 'Do not be afraid. From now on you will be catching people.' They pulled their boats up on the shore, left everything and followed him.",
 "Jesus chooses ordinary people: fishermen, a tax collector, a zealot. He does not wait until they are perfect. Peter's reaction is the right one: faced with Jesus' power and goodness, he knows his own weakness. Jesus does not leave him there; he says, 'Do not be afraid', and gives him a new mission. The Church describes the first apostles as the foundation of the Church, and Peter's boat has been a picture of the Church ever since. The Lord still calls all of us, in different ways, to follow him.",
 ['Why did the nets break?','What did Peter say, and what did Jesus say to him?','What might it mean to “leave your nets”?'],'Mark 1:17',hue=190)

s('cana','Water into Wine','NT',['John 2:1-11'],'🍷',
 "Jesus and his friends went to a wedding. The wine ran out! Mary said to the servants, 'Do whatever he tells you.' Jesus asked them to fill big jars with water. Then he turned the water into the best wine! It was Jesus' first miracle. Mary helped, and the servants did as they were asked.",
 "In the village of Cana in Galilee, there was a wedding feast, and Jesus, his mother Mary and his disciples were invited. Part way through, the wine ran out, which would have embarrassed the family. Mary quietly told Jesus, 'They have no wine.' Jesus said, 'My hour has not yet come.' But Mary turned to the servants and said, 'Do whatever he tells you.' Jesus asked them to fill six big stone jars with water. 'Now draw some out and take it to the master of the feast.' The master tasted it and was amazed: 'You have kept the best wine until now!' This was the first of Jesus' signs, and his disciples believed in him.",
 "John calls the miracles of Jesus 'signs': they point to who he is. At Cana, Jesus blesses a wedding, and shows that he brings abundance: six jars of about twenty gallons each, which is a huge amount of wine. Mary notices the need and brings it to Jesus; her only advice to us is 'Do whatever he tells you.' The servants' obedience, filling jars with water to the brim, is part of the miracle. The Church sees here a hint of the wedding of Christ and his Church and of the Eucharist, where Jesus gives us his blood under the form of wine. Marriage is a sacrament, and Jesus begins his public life at a wedding.",
 ['What did Mary notice?','What happened because the servants obeyed?','What does “Do whatever he tells you” mean for us?'],'John 2:5',hue=350)

s('beatitudes','The Sermon on the Mount','NT',['Matthew 5:1-16'],'⛰️',
 "Jesus sat on a hillside, and many people came to listen. He said, 'God blesses those who are gentle. God blesses those who are kind and who make peace. God blesses people with a pure heart.' Jesus said, 'You are the light of the world. Let your light shine!' Jesus tells us how to be truly happy.",
 "A big crowd followed Jesus, so he went up on a mountainside, sat down and began to teach. He gave the Beatitudes, which tell us who is truly blessed: the poor in spirit, those who are sad, the gentle, those who hunger and thirst for what is right, the merciful, the pure in heart, the peacemakers, and those who are treated badly for doing good. Jesus then said, 'You are the salt of the earth. You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and hides it under a basket. Let your light shine before others, so that they see the good you do and give glory to your Father in heaven.'",
 "The Sermon on the Mount is Jesus' great teaching on how to live. He climbs a mountain, as Moses did, but instead of commandments he starts with blessings. 'Blessed' means happy in the deepest way, the happiness that comes from God and not from comfort or power. The eight Beatitudes describe the life of the saints: poor in spirit, gentle, merciful, pure in heart, peacemakers. They turn the world's ideas upside down. Jesus then calls us salt and light: we are not asked to be impressive, but to be visible and useful. The Catechism calls the Beatitudes 'the heart of Jesus' preaching'.",
 ['Which Beatitude is your favourite?','How can you be a light at school or home?','Which saint was merciful, or a peacemaker?'],'Matthew 5:14',hue=120)

s('lords-prayer','Jesus Teaches Us to Pray','NT',['Matthew 6:5-15','Luke 11:1-4'],'🙏',
 "The friends of Jesus said, 'Teach us to pray.' Jesus said, 'Pray like this: Our Father in heaven, may your name be holy. Give us this day our daily bread. Forgive us, as we forgive others.' We still say this prayer today, the Our Father. We can pray to God as our loving Father.",
 "Jesus often went off by himself to pray. One day his disciples asked, 'Lord, teach us to pray.' Jesus said, 'When you pray, do not babble on to be heard, for your Father knows what you need before you ask him. Pray like this: Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name, your kingdom come, your will be done on earth as in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread, and forgive us our sins as we forgive those who sin against us. Lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil.' Jesus also said, 'If you forgive others, your Father will forgive you.'",
 "The Our Father is the prayer Jesus himself gave. It is short, and holds everything: praise (hallowed be your name), the longing for God's kingdom, the need for daily bread, forgiveness given and received, and protection from evil. Jesus invites us to call God 'Abba', 'Father', a word of closeness. The first petitions are about God, and only then do we ask for ourselves. Notice it is 'Our' Father, a prayer for the whole family of God, not just 'my' needs. The Catechism calls it 'the summary of the whole Gospel', and we pray it at every Mass before Communion.",
 ['What do we ask for in the Our Father?','Why is forgiveness part of the prayer?','When could you pray it slowly, a line at a time?'],'Matthew 6:9',hue=240)

s('paralytic','The Friends Who Lowered the Roof','NT',['Mark 2:1-12'],'🛏️',
 "A man could not walk. His four friends wanted Jesus to heal him. The house was too full to get in. So they went up on the roof and dug a hole! They lowered their friend down on his mat, right in front of Jesus. Jesus saw how much they believed. He said, 'Your sins are forgiven. Stand up and walk!' The man stood up and walked home.",
 "Jesus was teaching in a house in Capernaum, and the house was so crowded that no one else could get in, even at the door. Four men were carrying their paralysed friend on a mat. They could not get through the crowd, so they climbed up on the flat roof, made a hole, and lowered him down in front of Jesus. When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the man, 'Son, your sins are forgiven.' Some teachers of the law were upset: 'Only God can forgive sins!' Jesus said, 'So that you may know that I have authority on earth to forgive sins,' and he told the man, 'Get up, take your mat and go home.' The man got up at once and walked out. Everyone was amazed.",
 "Four friends refuse to let a crowd keep them from Jesus, and Jesus responds to *their* faith. Before healing the body, he forgives the man's sins: this shows what he thinks matters most. His claim to forgive sins is a claim to be God, and the critics rightly notice that, but they do not believe him. The healing is the sign that he really has the authority. This story is a reminder of the power of praying for others and bringing others to Jesus. It is also the Gospel behind the sacrament of Confession: through his priests Jesus still says, 'Your sins are forgiven.'",
 ['What did the friends do for the man?','Why did Jesus forgive his sins first?','Who could you bring to Jesus by praying for them?'],'Mark 2:5',hue=60)

s('storm','Jesus Calms the Storm','NT',['Mark 4:35-41'],'⛈️',
 "Jesus and his friends crossed the lake in a boat. Jesus was tired, and fell asleep. Then a big storm came! The waves splashed into the boat, and the friends were scared. 'Jesus, wake up!' Jesus stood up and said, 'Quiet! Be still!' The wind stopped, and the sea was calm. Jesus said, 'Why were you afraid?'",
 "One evening Jesus said to his disciples, 'Let us go across to the other side of the lake.' They set off in a boat, and Jesus fell asleep on a cushion in the back. A fierce storm blew up, and the waves broke over the boat so that it was nearly full of water. The disciples woke Jesus: 'Teacher, do you not care that we are going to drown?' Jesus got up and said to the wind and sea, 'Peace! Be still!' The wind dropped and there was a great calm. He asked them, 'Why are you so afraid? Do you still have no faith?' They were amazed and asked each other, 'Who is this? Even the wind and sea obey him!'",
 "The disciples include experienced fishermen, and they are terrified, so this was a real storm. The surprising detail is that Jesus is asleep in the boat: he is completely at peace in the Father's hands. When he wakes, he speaks to the storm as God spoke at creation, and the Psalms say that it is God who calms the roaring of the seas. The disciples' question, 'Who is this?', is the question of the whole Gospel. The Church has always seen the boat as an image of the Church, sometimes tossed about, but never abandoned: Jesus is in the boat.",
 ['What are you afraid of when storms come?','What did the disciples learn about Jesus?','How can we ask Jesus for help when we are scared?'],'Mark 4:39',hue=215)

s('five-thousand','Jesus Feeds the Crowd','NT',['John 6:1-15'],'🐟',
 "A huge crowd followed Jesus, and they were hungry. A little boy had five loaves of bread and two fish. He shared them with Jesus. Jesus thanked God and broke the bread. And the food kept going and going! Everyone ate until they were full, and there were twelve baskets left over. Sharing is good, and God gives more than enough.",
 "More than five thousand people had followed Jesus up the hill, and it was getting late. Jesus asked Philip, 'Where can we buy bread for these people to eat?' Philip said, 'Even eight months' wages would not buy enough!' Then Andrew said, 'There is a boy here with five barley loaves and two small fish, but what is that among so many?' Jesus told everyone to sit down on the grass. He took the loaves, gave thanks, and handed them out, and did the same with the fish. Everyone ate as much as they wanted. Afterwards Jesus said, 'Gather up the leftovers so nothing is wasted.' They filled twelve baskets.",
 "This is the only miracle told in all four Gospels. The disciples count what they lack; a boy offers what little he has; Jesus makes it enough. Look at the verbs: Jesus 'took, gave thanks, broke and gave', the same four actions as at the Last Supper and at every Mass. John tells us it was near Passover, and the next day Jesus preaches the Bread of Life discourse: 'I am the bread of life; whoever eats this bread will live for ever.' God will take our small offerings and multiply them. Twelve baskets, one for each apostle, hint that the Church will go on feeding the world.",
 ['What did the boy give?','What does Jesus do with the bread? Does it remind you of the Mass?','What small thing can you share this week?'],'John 6:35',hue=40)

s('walking-water','Jesus Walks on Water','NT',['Matthew 14:22-33'],'🚶',
 "The friends of Jesus were in a boat at night, and the wind blew hard. Then they saw Jesus walking on the water! 'Do not be afraid. It is me.' Peter said, 'Lord, let me come to you!' Peter stepped out and walked on the water. But when he looked at the big waves, he got scared and began to sink. Jesus took his hand: 'Why did you doubt?'",
 "After feeding the crowd, Jesus sent his disciples ahead in the boat while he went up the mountain to pray. In the night the boat was far from land and struggling against the wind. Just before dawn Jesus came toward them, walking on the lake. They cried out in fear: 'It is a ghost!' But Jesus said, 'Take courage! It is I. Do not be afraid.' Peter called, 'Lord, if it is you, tell me to come to you on the water.' 'Come,' said Jesus. Peter stepped out of the boat and began walking toward Jesus, but when he noticed the strong wind, he became frightened and began to sink. 'Lord, save me!' Jesus reached out and caught him. 'You of little faith, why did you doubt?' When they climbed into the boat, the wind stopped, and they worshipped him.",
 "Peter is the only one brave enough to ask to join Jesus on the waves, and for a short time he does the impossible, as long as his eyes are on Jesus. When he looks at the storm, he sinks. His prayer, 'Lord, save me!', is one of the shortest and best prayers in the Bible, and Jesus answers at once. The phrase 'It is I' can also be translated 'I AM', the name of God. The story shows Peter's impulsiveness, his faith and his weakness, which is why Jesus chooses him to lead. In the same way the Church, like the boat, is guided through storms by Peter's successors.",
 ['What made Peter sink?','How can we keep our eyes on Jesus?','What is a short prayer you can say when you are afraid?'],'Matthew 14:27',hue=200)
